## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a real-world KCNA pattern: dynamic routing controlled by a side-input, stateful enrichment with TTL, and delivery guarantees in the presence of config changes.

## Key Concepts

- State stores and TTL caches
- Side-input config streams
- Per-message offsets and at-least-once delivery
- ID-based deduplication across routing versions

## Code Example

```javascript
class RouterSink {
  constructor() {
    this.cache = new TTLCache({ ttl: 5 * 60 * 1000 }); // 5 minutes
  }

  onMessage(ctx, event) {
    const cached = this.cache.get(event.type);
    const route = cached?.route || 'default';

    emit({
      id: event.id,
      type: event.type,
      payload: event.payload,
      route,
      ts: event.ts
    });
  }

  onConfigUpdate(ctx, routingConfig) {
    for (const rule of routingConfig.rules) {
      this.cache.set(rule.eventType, { 
        route: rule.route, 
        version: routingConfig.version 
      });
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Fair sharing of resources across tenants is crucial in multi-tenant KCNA streams. This task validates per-tenant isolation, offset handling, and basic backpressure at beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant rate limiting, token bucket
- Cross-topic consumption and offset commits
- At-least-once semantics and fault tolerance
- Canary testing for bursts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: per-tenant limiter in consume loop
let buckets = new Map<string, {tokens:number, lastRefill:number}>();
function allow(tenantId){
  const now=Date.now();
  const b = buckets.get(tenantId) ?? {tokens:0, lastRefill:now};
  const elapsed=(now-b.lastRefill)/1000;
  b.tokens = Math.min(200, b.tokens + 100*elapsed);
  b.lastRefill = now;
  buckets.set(tenantId,b);
  if(b.tokens>=1){ b.tokens-=1; return true;} return false;
}
```

